WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.483
What type of story scares you the&nbsp;
most?

00:00:06.483 --> 00:00:11.219
Perhaps something dark, visceral, rooted in physical danger?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:16.740
Or something subtle,&nbsp;
psychological, with layers of unseen dread? 

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.620
Or maybe something unknowable, inexplicable,&nbsp;
that lifts the veil on reality and exposes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:29.580
the cosmic terrors of the universe? The work of&nbsp;
Japanese horror manga artist Junji Ito exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:36.300
at the intersection of all our greatest fears.&nbsp;
His tales capture primal anxieties so expertly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:42.360
they can seem truly mysterious. So, what I&nbsp;
want to do today is peel back those layers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:49.320
of mystery, and explore what makes Junji Ito’s&nbsp;
worldbuilding so… darn spooky. And to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.180
we’ll need to find our courage and dive&nbsp;
headfirst into this ocean of terrors…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:03.240
Our first specimen from the inky depths of&nbsp;
Junji Ito’s imagination is a fish with strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.840
spider-like legs. The creature comes from the&nbsp;
story “Gyo” — a narrative famous for being both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:17.160
deeply innovative and utterly unhinged. The tale&nbsp;
begins with a young man named Tadashi enjoying a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:24.120
vacation in Okinawa, Japan. Returning from a scuba&nbsp;
trip, he discovers at his house a strange fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:30.960
possessing what appear to be metallic legs. There&nbsp;
is no indication of where such a baffling lifeform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.960
could have come from, with Tadashi wondering if&nbsp;
it’s an undiscovered species. But this creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.780
is just the beginning... On the beach later that&nbsp;
night, more fish scuttle out of the water on long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:48.780
spindly limbs — a visual that is equal&nbsp;
parts absurd and unsettling. These fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:56.340
however, are merely scouts: the first wave of&nbsp;
an incomprehensible invasion. Across Okinawa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.900
sightings of limbed marine life become&nbsp;
more frequent. On a fishing trawler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:07.020
a day’s haul turns deadly and attacks the&nbsp;
crew. The entire ocean seems changed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:12.780
an ecosystem of ravenous terrors, with&nbsp;
no species left unaffected. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:18.720
the narrative escalates at a breakneck pace,&nbsp;
with an army of fish sweeping over the land like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.080
a great wave. Species from higher and higher up&nbsp;
the oceanic food chain venture out of the depths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:31.500
a trend that eventually results in a massive shark&nbsp;
rampaging across the surface. Beyond the obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.720
horror of a land shark trying to eat you, there’s&nbsp;
an uncomfortable sense of shattered boundaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.240
in this scene — a feeling that you’re seeing&nbsp;
something that simply should not be. Terrors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.820
from the sea are a familiar trope, coming with set&nbsp;
expectations as to when the monster can strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.060
and when characters are safe. But “Gyo” destroys&nbsp;
that barrier of safety, eliminating all places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.760
to hide from the creatures of the abyss. The&nbsp;
violent collision of realms creates a tangible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.980
helplessness, a certainty of extinction&nbsp;
central to Junji Ito’s style of horror.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.900
…There’s another story about dark things emerging&nbsp;
from the depths, one that can help us better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:19.800
understand the madness of Junji Ito’s “Gyo.”&nbsp;
The surreal video game “Death Stranding” is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.600
set in the wake of a cataclysm where entities&nbsp;
known as “beached things” entered our world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.540
And though the creatures emerged not from the sea&nbsp;
but from a purgatory-like realm called the Beach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.840
the parallels between the apocalypses of “Death&nbsp;
Stranding” and “Gyo” are illuminating. Both are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.780
stories of worlds colliding — of incompatible&nbsp;
environments smashing together with catastrophic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.840
consequences. The clashing of worlds can be felt&nbsp;
in designs that merge the mechanical with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:55.020
biological — the incongruent elements forced&nbsp;
together in a way that seems almost… painful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.940
There’s a sense that the marine life in “Gyo”&nbsp;
and the beached things in “Death Stranding”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.400
didn’t ask to be dragged ashore, and are just&nbsp;
as much victims of this collision as humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:12.900
One lesser-known short story of Junji Ito’s,&nbsp;
titled “The Thing That Drifted Ashore,” dives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:18.600
deeper into the horror and tragedy of a lifeform&nbsp;
stranded out of its element — and is the missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.900
puzzle piece in understanding how Ito and “Death&nbsp;
Stranding” fit together. The narrative begins with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:31.200
the corpse of a massive creature washing up on the&nbsp;
shores of the Pacific Ocean. It resembles a mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.780
serpent, although decompression from rising from&nbsp;
the abyss has turned it into something grotesque.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:43.140
Its body is bloated and rotting, with a head&nbsp;
covered in tumors and parasites. A crowd has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:49.260
come to watch this shipwrecked creature decompose,&nbsp;
among them a boy with a strange connection to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:55.500
sea who serves as the narrator. For much of his&nbsp;
life, the boy has been tormented with dreams of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:01.320
the deep ocean — cursed with distressing visions&nbsp;
of great fish that swim through the hostile dark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:07.080
The dreams started following a traumatic trip&nbsp;
to an aquarium, where the boy witnessed deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.840
sea fish his mind wasn’t ready to perceive. In&nbsp;
this regard, the boy and the beached creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:18.540
have a peculiar connection, with both suffering&nbsp;
from their exposure to the other’s environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.420
The theme of environmental suffering in “The&nbsp;
Thing That Drifted Ashore” is reminiscent of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:29.520
real tragic phenomenon. Throughout history,&nbsp;
whales and other marine mammals have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.920
observed beaching themselves, then collapsing&nbsp;
under their own weight. The phenomenon seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:40.740
have worsened due to the invention of sonar, and&nbsp;
is known as ‘Cetacean Stranding’ — which, notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:46.380
is where “Death Stranding” gets its title from.&nbsp;
On a surface level, the beached things in “Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.260
Stranding” parallel the marooned creatures of&nbsp;
Junji Ito and Earth’s real shores. Yet the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:57.840
survivors inhabiting the post-apocalyptic world&nbsp;
of the game are also ‘marooned’ in their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.900
way. In the wake of the cataclysmic clash of&nbsp;
realities, Earth has been transformed into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:10.680
inhospitable land reminiscent of the sea floor.&nbsp;
Like marine life on dry land, humans suffer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.860
this new environment, their bodies not built for&nbsp;
such an ecosystem… In the latter half of “The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:22.380
Thing That Drifted Ashore,” the onlookers notice&nbsp;
something inside the creature’s stomach. Through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.814
a layer of transparent skin, human beings stare&nbsp;
back at the crowd. Cutting the creature open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.814 --> 00:06:28.980
the onlookers are shocked to discover the people&nbsp;
are still alive. The survivors of a boat accident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:35.100
from seven years prior, they somehow became&nbsp;
zombie-like parasites living in the intestines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:41.340
of the leviathan after being swallowed. Like&nbsp;
the boy traumatized by the aquarium, the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:47.160
of staring into the abyss through the creature’s&nbsp;
transparent skin caused them to lose their minds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.400
There are some things humans are not meant&nbsp;
to witness. Things our mind cannot fathom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:07:01.740
that defy all reason. The crowd in “The Thing”&nbsp;
attempt to rationalize the unknown creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:06.660
theorizing how it might have moved through the&nbsp;
depths — yet are ultimately unable to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:13.080
it. Likewise, multiple explanations for the limbed&nbsp;
fish are put forward in “Gyo,” from a military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:19.440
experiment gone wrong to a natural phenomenon,&nbsp;
but none seems quite right. A central element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:25.020
of what makes Junji Ito’s horror worldbuilding&nbsp;
so effective is how much he withholds from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:30.120
reader. Without a clear sense of how a threat&nbsp;
functions, your rational mind goes blank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.380
letting you experience terrors on&nbsp;
a primal level. In some respects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:40.200
the creator of “Death Stranding,” Hideo Kojima,&nbsp;
takes the opposite approach, as his game has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:46.860
hours upon hours dedicated to explaining how its&nbsp;
universe works. And side note: Junji Ito himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.860
actually makes a cameo in one of these expository&nbsp;
sequences, making his influence on the game more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:59.340
evident. But Junji Ito’s own horror rarely grants&nbsp;
the comfort of an explanation. Like staring into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:04.920
the depths of the sea, reading his work can feel&nbsp;
like gazing at something truly unfathomable...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:13.620
And perhaps no story wields the terror of the&nbsp;
inexplicable better “The Enigma of Amigara&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:20.460
Fault.” The setup is deceptively simple: an&nbsp;
earthquake reveals a several kilometer long fault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:26.220
and upon the rockface, foreboding holes shaped&nbsp;
like the silhouettes of humans become visible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:31.680
People from across the nation are drawn to the&nbsp;
unfathomable sight after seeing it on the news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.920
Each of the thousands of holes appears to extend&nbsp;
endlessly into the dark of the mountain. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:45.000
no indication as to where they came from. They&nbsp;
simply are. And then, things get stranger. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:51.000
in the crowd start to realize that certain holes&nbsp;
are shaped exactly like them. And one by one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.820
they are overcome with a compulsion to enter&nbsp;
their holes, disappearing into the suffocating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:01.860
depths. There’s an unbearable sense of&nbsp;
claustrophobia this story can instill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:07.380
making it impossible not to imagine what it would&nbsp;
be like to be stuck within the holes yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:13.620
unable to move. Beyond this physical terror,&nbsp;
there’s real psychological dread in the way people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:19.560
can’t escape the impulse to trap themselves, their&nbsp;
agency slipping away as the inexplicable need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:27.180
becomes all-consuming. It’s really grim stuff.&nbsp;
And maybe… kind of funny? For as disturbing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:32.940
the situation can be, there’s an absurdity to the&nbsp;
concept that can at times feel almost comedic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:39.180
This humor is present in the margins of a lot of&nbsp;
Junji Ito’s work, and can at first be difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:45.600
reconcile with the disturbing subject matter. But&nbsp;
understanding how absurdity and fear intertwine is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.780
central to appreciating why these stories are so&nbsp;
affecting — and to do that, we have to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.780 --> 00:10:06.120
“House.” [Sounds of Chaos] Directed by Nobuhiko&nbsp;
Obayashi, “House” is… a lot. A psychedelic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:11.460
blend of horror and comedy, the film involves a&nbsp;
group of friends visiting the mansion of an old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:17.940
woman in the remote countryside. This seemingly&nbsp;
standard plot soon derails into total insanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:24.180
as common household objects attack the group, the&nbsp;
effects designed to look fake as possible. A piano&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:30.420
eats someone, another character has a fight with&nbsp;
sentient futon cushions and loses, one person gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:36.960
teleported inside an evil clock. It can seem like&nbsp;
nonsense, and a lot of it is — but like the works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:43.020
of Junji Ito, there’s a method to the madness. The&nbsp;
silliness makes the rare moments of genuine terror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:48.060
more visceral and unexpected. Because you’re&nbsp;
never sure what kind of scene you’ll get next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:54.720
it’s hard to relax. Compared to Ito’s horror, this&nbsp;
film definitely leans harder into absurdity, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.760
the point where it can outright break your brain.&nbsp;
There’s a scene where a pair of disembodied legs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:05.160
kick a cat painting, which destroys the spirit of&nbsp;
a cat, because the cat’s soul was in the painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.960
or it was the painting, but then the cat was&nbsp;
also a part of the lady who owns the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:15.300
so she’s destroyed, but then it turns out, not&nbsp;
really. Do not ask me what is happening because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.329 --> 00:11:22.909
sound like they were written by a child… they&nbsp;
were. Obayashi used story ideas from his young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.909 --> 00:11:28.309
daughter, as he believed the irrational thoughts&nbsp;
of a child would help the movie frighten people on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.309 --> 00:11:35.029
a more intrinsic level. Yes, much of “House” is&nbsp;
absurd — but ultimately, all absurdity means is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:39.829
a lack of reason, and if you can’t reason&nbsp;
with something, you aren’t safe from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.849 --> 00:11:47.209
At the end of “The Enigma of Amigara Fault,” every&nbsp;
main character succumbs to the need to enter the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.209 --> 00:11:53.209
hole they were made for. Months later, a research&nbsp;
team discovers the fault extends to the other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.209 --> 00:11:58.909
of the mountain — but the holes are no longer&nbsp;
the same shape. And from the darkness, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.909 --> 00:12:05.329
horrifically twisted is creeping slowly closer.&nbsp;
…And that’s the last glimpse you get. You’re left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.329 --> 00:12:11.809
with no explanations. No resolution. Just the&nbsp;
terrifying, bewildering absurdity of it all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.509 --> 00:12:20.209
…If there’s a culmination to Junji Ito’s work,&nbsp;
an ultimate merger of fear and absurdity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.209 --> 00:12:25.609
I’d say it’s probably a little story&nbsp;
titled “Uzumaki.” I say ‘little’ story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.609 --> 00:12:31.789
but “Uzumaki” is one of the longest Ito ever&nbsp;
wrote, and in my opinion, ultimately the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.789 --> 00:12:38.569
unsettling. The narrative is centered on a city&nbsp;
plagued by a mysterious, supernatural curse that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.569 --> 00:12:45.649
takes the form of… spirals. Yes, spirals. Every&nbsp;
scare in the story links back in some way or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.649 --> 00:12:51.829
another to this simple pattern. And if that sounds&nbsp;
too strange to be frightening… well, wait and see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.489 --> 00:12:58.969
The spirals start small. A man becomes obsessed&nbsp;
with the whirl of a snail’s shell, staring at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.969 --> 00:13:05.209
for hours. Tiny whirlpools appear in the city’s&nbsp;
water supply. But soon, people’s bodies begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.209 --> 00:13:11.329
to horribly contort into the spiral shape. The&nbsp;
pattern spreads like a disease, with everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.329 --> 00:13:17.089
in the city becoming infected. Buildings loop&nbsp;
back onto each other like a swirling labyrinth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.089 --> 00:13:24.229
Plants grow in endless, dizzying twirls. Even&nbsp;
the sky itself becomes a horrible spiral, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.229 --> 00:13:30.289
clouds becoming a dark omen of the city’s fate.&nbsp;
One of the most discussed elements of “Uzumaki”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.289 --> 00:13:36.049
is how helpless the main characters are in the&nbsp;
face of such a cosmic, incomprehensible threat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.049 --> 00:13:42.049
Like the shape itself, there’s an inevitability&nbsp;
to the spirals — a sense from the beginning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.049 --> 00:13:48.109
things will only get worse and worse. The story&nbsp;
conveys a level of powerlessness very few pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.109 --> 00:13:59.029
of media have achieved. [Music] If “Uzumaki” has a&nbsp;
companion, another narrative experience that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.029 --> 00:14:04.789
you feel similarly insignificant in the face of&nbsp;
unknowable dangers, I’d argue it’s the indie game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.789 --> 00:14:11.149
“World of Horror.” Created by Polish developer&nbsp;
Pawel Kozminski, the point and click adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:17.569
is all about making the player feel as defenseless&nbsp;
as possible. In the story, you solve mysteries in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.569 --> 00:14:23.629
a city afflicted by enigmatic entities known&nbsp;
as the old gods. The plot and aesthetics are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.629 --> 00:14:29.029
explicitly inspired by the work of Junji Ito, and&nbsp;
playthroughs really can feel like you’re living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.029 --> 00:14:34.129
through one of his stories. You’re constantly on&nbsp;
the backfoot, trying to manage your character’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.129 --> 00:14:40.249
dwindling sanity and stamina as you experience a&nbsp;
never-ending parade of nightmares in a randomly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.249 --> 00:14:46.249
generated order. It’s not really a game that&nbsp;
you win so much as a game that you… undergo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.249 --> 00:14:53.809
Play for long enough, and some form of dark fate&nbsp;
is virtually inevitable. I don’t think it’s much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.809 --> 00:15:00.349
of a spoiler to say that “Uzumaki” has a similar&nbsp;
ending. As is the case across Junji Ito’s stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.349 --> 00:15:06.289
there’s no winning against something so irrational&nbsp;
it’s impossible to understand. In the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.289 --> 00:15:12.529
chapter, there’s some explanation as to where the&nbsp;
spirals come from, but it’s so vague it functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.529 --> 00:15:18.529
less like an answer and more like another&nbsp;
question — another layer in an infinite mystery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:28.249
Balancing ambiguity and irrationality with genuine&nbsp;
fear is a tricky thing. Junji Ito pushes these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.249 --> 00:15:33.649
concepts near their breaking point in “Hellstar&nbsp;
Remina,” an absolutely bonkers narrative about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.649 --> 00:15:38.989
a planet that appears in Earth’s solar system&nbsp;
out of blue. What at first seems like a passing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.989 --> 00:15:45.229
celestial object turns out to be something else&nbsp;
entirely, as the planet opens a single giant eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.229 --> 00:15:51.649
and beholds the Earth. Ito’s reoccurring theme of&nbsp;
humanity’s insignificance is perhaps at its most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.649 --> 00:15:57.529
extreme in these panels, with Earth rendered&nbsp;
a meaningless speck in a cruel cosmos. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.529 --> 00:16:04.009
attempts by humans to understand the interstellar&nbsp;
visitor end in disaster, and mass hysteria grips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.009 --> 00:16:10.249
the entire population. And from there, well,&nbsp;
let’s just say that the rogue planet turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.249 --> 00:16:15.649
to have a rather large appetite. It’s some truly&nbsp;
out-there media, to the point where it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.649 --> 00:16:21.469
be too much for some people. But Junji Ito’s&nbsp;
content is meant to be ‘out-there’ — meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.469 --> 00:16:27.289
be so surreal and at times even ludicrous that&nbsp;
you can’t anticipate what happens next. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.289 --> 00:16:33.169
Junji Ito’s more lighthearted works is his “Cat&nbsp;
Diary” — an autobiographical story about getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.169 --> 00:16:38.509
over his fear of his wife’s cats. And while&nbsp;
nothing disturbing happens in the actual plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.509 --> 00:16:44.029
the art is still so comically unsettling —&nbsp;
with the ordinary cats portrayed like they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.029 --> 00:16:50.269
cosmic terrors. To me, this is Ito reminding us&nbsp;
that the absurdity of his horror is a feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.269 --> 00:16:57.829
not a bug — an essential part of what makes his&nbsp;
work so consistently unpredictable. Junji Ito’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.829 --> 00:17:03.949
stories are an exercise in tonal contradictions,&nbsp;
which is likely why his work is so hard to adapt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.949 --> 00:17:09.349
Despite many attempts, most translations of his&nbsp;
narratives to other mediums feel like they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:15.109
missing something. Even something as simple as&nbsp;
adding color to his illustrations can throw off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:21.829
his delicate balance of ambiguity. That’s why one&nbsp;
of my favorite adaptations is probably “Mold,” an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:26.989
episode from a recent Netflix anthology that&nbsp;
keeps things in black and white. The original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.989 --> 00:17:33.049
short story tells the tale of someone returning&nbsp;
to an old house only to find a nauseating mold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.049 --> 00:17:39.049
has taken over the foundations. The lack of color&nbsp;
is a huge part of what makes the art so effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.049 --> 00:17:45.229
as it’s difficult to tell what the mold really&nbsp;
is. The episode’s choice to keep the color drained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.229 --> 00:17:50.989
from each scene preserves that uncertainty, that&nbsp;
creeping sense that something else is going on in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.989 --> 00:17:57.529
the house. The visuals work so well, I honestly&nbsp;
wonder if all adaptations of Junji Ito shouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.529 --> 00:18:03.529
stick to black and white. Adult Swim has been&nbsp;
working on a pen and ink adaptation of “Uzumaki”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.529 --> 00:18:09.889
for a long, long time, and I sincerely hope it’s&nbsp;
as good as early footage makes it seem. For the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.889 --> 00:18:15.409
dream of a perfect adaptation that recaptures&nbsp;
everything that makes Junji Ito’s art so special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.409 --> 00:18:23.509
is difficult to let go of. But ultimately, Junji&nbsp;
Ito’s work is irreplaceable for a reason. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.509 --> 00:18:28.909
artist has been quoted as saying that he’ll spend&nbsp;
multiple days refining a single panel, making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.909 --> 00:18:34.549
the image perfectly captures the terror of what&nbsp;
he’s trying to convey. And you can feel that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.549 --> 00:18:40.309
every line — that relentless attention to detail&nbsp;
that makes his art so rewarding to revisit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.309 --> 00:18:46.489
analyze. Yet at the same time, his stories often&nbsp;
don’t take themselves too seriously, reminding us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.489 --> 00:18:52.729
that absurdity is just as much a part of life as&nbsp;
bodily horror. In the end, like a pair of severed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.729 --> 00:18:58.729
legs kicking a cat painting, Junji Ito’s art&nbsp;
is a deeply original blend of fear, silliness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.729 --> 00:19:06.949
ambiguity and irrationality — as difficult to&nbsp;
summarize as it is to duplicate. As always, thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.949 --> 00:19:12.949
for watching. If you enjoyed this entry, please&nbsp;
lend your support by liking, subscribing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.949 --> 00:19:19.009
hitting the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

